Overlay patch 0055 (VR R18 part B): THE LENS OF TRUTH, IN A HEADSET.

the user, on 1.0.1.22: "I select it; it should show the same red interface with a
circle like vanilla."

WHAT WAS ALREADY TRUE, and had to be established before writing a line: the lens
mask IS drawn in VR, per eye, and no gate skips it. Actor_DrawLensOverlay /
Actor_DrawLensActors (this file) emit two gSPWideTextureRectangle of the 64x64 I8
gLensOfTruthMaskTex into POLY_XLU_DISP -- the WORLD list -- so overlay 0043's HUD
detachment never touches them, and the toggle itself is reachable in VR through
overlay 0051's item compass writing the item's own C button. Three things,
none of them "the lens is missing", made it not read as vanilla's lens:

  (i)  IT WAS AN OVAL WITH CLIPPED SIDES. That is overlay 0031 rev20's fix and
       not this patch's: the display list is built while mCurDimensions is still
       the HUD pass's 4:3 1600x1200, so OTRGetRectDimensionFromLeftEdge(0) is 0
       and the rect already spans the whole field -- and then
       AdjXForAspectRatio multiplied its edges by (4/3)/eye_aspect anyway. See
       0031's header; that fix un-stretches every world-list screen-space rect
       and the world's own geometry with it.
  (ii) THE TINT READ AS NOTHING. Vanilla's mask alpha peaks at 74/255 and its
       combiner (G_CC_MODULATEIA_PRIM) spends that same 74 on the COLOUR too, so
       the ring is a 29 %-opaque 29 %-bright red. On a television that is a
       tint; over a ~1900 px eye at 100 degrees it is nearly invisible.
  (iii) IT HANDED THE COMPOSITOR A NEAR-PLANE FIELD. Vanilla's first pass sets
       gDPSetPrimDepth(0, 0) -- the NEAR plane -- with Z_UPD across everything
       the mask covers, because that is how it clips the invisible actors to the
       circle. 0031 rev13 publishes the eye's DEPTH texture to the compositor
       for reprojection, so with the lens on, the compositor was reprojecting a
       large part of the view as if it were centimetres from the wearer's face.

THE OVERLAY THIS PATCH ADDS: Actor_VrDrawLensOverlay, branched in at BOTH call
sites of Actor_DrawLensOverlay -- the depth pass and the colour pass -- so the
hole in the depth mask and the hole in the tint are the SAME hole by
construction. #ifdef __IOS__, and behaviourally gated on
`gSohVRMode != 0 && gSohVRFlatLatch == 0`, the same predicate every other VR
overlay uses for "this frame's list belongs in the world". Everything else --
the oracle, iPhone, VR's own flat-screen frames, the pause menu -- takes
vanilla's function, byte for byte.

GEOMETRY, DERIVED RATHER THAN TUNED. One rect, from
OTRGetRectDimensionFromLeftEdge(0) to OTRGetRectDimensionFromRightEdge(
SCREEN_WIDTH): those two helpers are exactly the pair whose output spans the
field edge to edge under whatever aspect the list was built with, which is why
using them (rather than a hard 0..SCREEN_WIDTH) keeps this correct with the HUD
plane on OR off -- `vr set hudplane 0` is the A/B for 0031's half and must not
change the lens's half. The circle is kept ROUND in the eye by scaling s and t
independently:

    Dv = 2*LENS_MASK_HEIGHT * SCREEN_HEIGHT / (SCREEN_HEIGHT - 2*LENS_MASK_OFFSET_T)
    Dh = Dv * built_aspect / eye_aspect

-- Dv is vanilla's OWN vertical diameter re-derived from its own constants (128
texels of tile spread over 140 of the 240 screen rows), and Dh is the width that
makes the circle physically round given that the field is `eye_aspect` wide and
1 tall while its coordinates are `built_aspect` wide and 1 tall. The tile is
centred on the field centre by arithmetic (the tile's own s/t centre minus the
rate times the distance to the rect's left/top edge), so nothing here is a
magic offset. `vr set lensscale` (0.3 .. 2.0, default 1.0 = vanilla's
proportion) exists because a headset's field is far wider than a television's
and no one has WORN vanilla's proportion in one.

THE TINT: `vr set lenstint` (1.0 .. 6.0, default 2.2), realised as repeated
passes of the same rect, with a combiner that spends the mask on the ALPHA only
and takes the colour from PRIMITIVE:

    colour = PRIMITIVE                       (a real red, not 29 % of one)
    alpha  = TEXEL0 * PRIMITIVE              (the mask, times this pass's alpha)

so opacity accumulates as 1 - (1 - a)^n: 29 % at vanilla, about 53 % at the
shipped 2.2. THE CENTRE STAYS CLEAR BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by clamping --
the mask is 0 inside the circle, G_AC_THRESHOLD discards a zero-alpha fragment,
and zero stays zero however many times it is drawn. The FIRST pass is always
full alpha, which matters at the depth site: it is the pass whose fragments
write the depth mask, and it must cover exactly what vanilla's covered.

THE DEPTH (item iii): `vr set lenszfar` (default 1). AFTER the invisible-actor
loop and the colour pass -- i.e. after everything that USES vanilla's near-plane
mask has used it -- the same masked region is re-written at prim depth 0x7FFF,
the FAR plane, through vanilla's own "update the z-buffer but not the color
frame buffer" mode (which Fast3D recognises as its `invisible` shader option, so
no pixel changes colour). The actor clipping is untouched because it happened
first; what changes is only what the compositor is handed. HONEST COST, and it
is why there is a dial: vanilla's near-plane field also rejects anything drawn
LATER in the frame at those pixels, and a far field lets such a draw through. 0
restores vanilla exactly.

Storage rule as ever: every gSohVRLens* global is DEFINED in app/ios/SohIosShell.m
and only externed here, so no overlay owns storage and the oracle links.

REV 2 -- R19 PART B (D-073): THE RECT WAS DRAWN TWICE AND FUSED NOWHERE.

the user, on 1.0.1.23: "I see doubled red circles in my eyes; it should be ONE
oval open space surrounded by red."

Two circles is what a SCREEN-SPACE rect must produce in this headset, and the
reason is arithmetic in two parts:

  (a) ZERO DISPARITY. A texrect lands at the same PIXELS in both eyes. Overlay
      0031's Soh3DStereoP substitutes the per-eye view*projection at the MP
      sites -- it never touches an orthographic screen-space draw -- so eye 0
      and eye 1 get identical pixels. Identical pixels fuse at INFINITY, while
      everything else in the frame fuses at its own distance. The wearer's
      vergence cannot serve both, so one of them doubles.
  (b) THE CENTRE OF THE FIELD IS NOT THE GAZE AXIS. This device's per-eye frusta
      are strongly asymmetric -- measured on the drawable, the left eye's
      tangents are (L -1.7321, R 1.0), i.e. NDC x = 0 sits about 8 degrees
      temporal of straight ahead, and the right eye's asymmetry is the MIRROR of
      it. A rect centred on the field is therefore centred several degrees off
      axis in OPPOSITE directions per eye: the two images are not merely at the
      wrong depth, they are pulled apart horizontally.

THE FIX IS TO STOP DRAWING IN SCREEN SPACE. Actor_VrLensQuad draws the same mask
as a HEAD-LOCKED WORLD QUAD in POLY_XLU at gSohVRLensDist metres (default 1.5)
in front of the head, using the head pose the shell already publishes in GAME
units for exactly this class of problem (gSohVRCamEye / gSohVRCamFwd /
gSohVRCamUp / gSohVRCamValid, overlay 0037's unification). Both eyes then look
at the SAME WORLD POINT, so the circle fuses where the wearer's eyes converge
and lands on the gaze axis in both eyes BY CONSTRUCTION -- the per-eye frustum
asymmetry is now the projection's business, which is precisely where it belongs.
The basis is fully 3D (right = normalize(fwd x up), up2 = right x fwd), so a
head ROLL rotates the quad with the head and the circle stays a circle. The mask
is radially symmetric, so the handedness of that cross product is unobservable
-- a mirrored circle is the same circle.

THE SIZE, AND THE ONE NUMBER THAT WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. The mask's
circle is not the whole texture. Reading the shipped resource out of the
extracted archive (objects/gameplay_keep/gLensOfTruthMaskTex, 64x64 I8, value 74
outside the circle and 0 inside, MIRROR|CLAMP so the 64 texels are a QUARTER of
the mask with the circle's centre at the corner (63,63)), the 74->0 boundary
sits at radius 54.2 .. 55.1 texels, i.e. **54.8**, sampled every fourth row.
That is the number the screen-space rect never had to know, because it inherited
vanilla's rates. In tile coordinates the mirror centre is 63.5 and the tile is
128 texels, which vanilla spreads over Dv = 2*64*240/140 = 219.43 screen rows,
so vanilla's circle radius is 54.8/128 * 219.43 = 93.95 rows out of the 120-row
half field: **0.7828 of the vertical half field**, which is the proportion this
quad reproduces:

    D    = clamp(gSohVRLensDist, 0.4, 4.0) * gSohVRWorldScale     (game units)
    tv   = max(|tanT|, |tanB|) over both eyes  (gSohVREyeTan, published by the
           shell this round; 1.0 if it has not arrived)
    r_c  = 0.7828 * D * tv * gSohVRLensScale                      (game units)
    H    = 3.0 * D                        (the quad's half extent; oversize)
    k    = 54.8 / r_c                     (texels per game unit)
    tc   = (63.5 +/- H*k - 0.5 + tile origin) << 5                (S10.5)

H is deliberately three times the distance -- far larger than the frustum's own
1.73 * D -- because the tile is MIRROR|CLAMP and Fast3D's clamp is a shader
clamp to the tile's 128 texels, so everything past the circle is the same flat
red field and oversizing it simply guarantees no edge of the quad can enter the
view at any head roll. The 0.5 is Fast3D's own bilerp offset, which it adds to a
TRIANGLE's texture coordinates and not to a rect's (interpreter.cpp: "Linear
filter adds 0.5f to the coordinates ... if (!is_rect)"). H is capped so the
largest texture coordinate stays inside s16 at the tightest legal dials
(lensscale 0.3 with a narrow field), and the cap is far away from anything a
real headset produces.

BOTH CALL SITES AND THE FAR-DEPTH WRITE GO THROUGH THE SAME QUAD, because the
branch is the first thing Actor_VrDrawLensOverlay does. That is not tidiness: the
depth pass writes PRIM depth (G_ZS_PRIM), so the geometry it is drawn with
changes only its FOOTPRINT, and routing it through the quad makes the hole in
the depth mask coincide with the visible circle PER EYE by construction. A
screen-space depth mask with a world-space tint would have clipped the invisible
actors to a circle that is not where either eye sees one.

THE KNOWN COST, SAID OUT LOUD: the head pose is sampled when the display list is
BUILT, i.e. at the game's tick rate, and the quad's anchor rides a recorded
matrix so frame interpolation glides it -- but interpolation is a lerp between
two sampled poses, not a fresh pose, so a fast head turn can make the vignette
swim slightly behind the head. If that reads badly in the headset the fallback
is a shell-side Metal disc composited after the eye pass (which needs a depth
state that does not write depth); it is NOT implemented this round, and the
one-command A/B is `vr set lensworld 0`, which restores the screen-space rect
byte for byte.


--- a/soh/src/code/z_actor.c
+++ b/soh/src/code/z_actor.c
@@ -2854,10 +2854,455 @@
     gSPWideTextureRectangle(POLY_XLU_DISP++, 0, 0, w, SCREEN_HEIGHT << 2, G_TX_RENDERTILE, LENS_MASK_OFFSET_S << 5,
                             LENS_MASK_OFFSET_T << 5, (1 << 10) * (SCREEN_WIDTH - 2 * LENS_MASK_OFFSET_S) / SCREEN_WIDTH,
                             (1 << 10) * (SCREEN_HEIGHT - 2 * LENS_MASK_OFFSET_T) / SCREEN_HEIGHT);
+    gDPPipeSync(POLY_XLU_DISP++);
+    CLOSE_DISPS(gfxCtx);
+}
+
+#ifdef __IOS__
+/* SOH_IOS (overlay 0055, VR R18 part B, D-071): THE LENS OF TRUTH IN VR.
+ *
+ * the user, on 1.0.1.22: "I select it; it should show the same red interface with
+ * a circle like vanilla."
+ *
+ * The mask was never missing -- it is drawn per eye out of the WORLD list, which
+ * overlay 0043's HUD detachment does not touch. What was wrong was its SHAPE
+ * (fixed in overlay 0031 rev20, which corrects AdjXForAspectRatio to the aspect
+ * the display list was BUILT with instead of the pass being rendered), its
+ * STRENGTH (74/255 of alpha AND of colour, which over a ~1900 px eye is very
+ * nearly nothing), and the NEAR-PLANE depth field it left for the compositor to
+ * reproject. This function is the second and third of those. See the patch
+ * header for the derivation of every number below.
+ *
+ * Every one of these globals is DEFINED in app/ios/SohIosShell.m. */
+extern volatile int gSohVRMode;
+extern volatile int gSohVRFlatLatch;
+extern volatile int gSoh3DEyeW, gSoh3DEyeH;
+extern volatile int gSohVRLensZFar;
+extern volatile int gSohVRLensDraws;
+extern volatile int gSohVRLensPasses;
+extern volatile float gSohVRLensTint;
+extern volatile float gSohVRLensScale;
+/* R19 part B (D-073): the mask as a head-locked WORLD quad. */
+extern volatile int gSohVRLensWorld;
+extern volatile float gSohVRLensDist;
+extern volatile float gSohVRWorldScale;
+extern volatile int gSohVRCamValid;
+extern volatile float gSohVRCamEye[3];
+extern volatile float gSohVRCamFwd[3];
+extern volatile float gSohVRCamUp[3];
+extern volatile float gSohVREyeTan[2][4]; /* [eye][L,R,B,T] */
+
+/* VR GAMEPLAY, not merely VR: gSohVRFlatLatch is the same predicate overlays
+ * 0031, 0039 and 0043 use for "this frame's display list belongs in the world
+ * rather than on the flat panel". A menu frame keeps vanilla's rect. */
+static s32 Actor_VrLensActive(void) {
+    return (gSohVRMode != 0) && (gSohVRFlatLatch == 0);
+}
+
+/* ------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ * R19 part B (D-073): THE MASK AS A HEAD-LOCKED WORLD QUAD.
+ *
+ * the user, on 1.0.1.23: "I see doubled red circles in my eyes; it should be ONE
+ * oval open space surrounded by red." A screen-space texrect lands at identical
+ * PIXELS in both eyes (overlay 0031 substitutes the per-eye view*projection at
+ * the MP sites and never touches an orthographic draw), so it carries zero
+ * disparity and fuses at infinity -- and this device's per-eye frusta are
+ * asymmetric, so the centre of the field is several degrees off the gaze axis in
+ * OPPOSITE directions per eye. Two circles, pulled apart. Drawn in the WORLD at
+ * gSohVRLensDist metres instead, both eyes see the SAME POINT and the circle
+ * fuses on the gaze axis by construction. See the patch header for every number
+ * below, including the 54.8-texel circle radius measured off the shipped
+ * gLensOfTruthMaskTex resource.
+ * --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
+/* The mask tile: 128 texels of MIRROR|CLAMP whose mirror centre is 63.5, with
+ * the circle's 74->0 boundary at 54.8 texels from that centre (measured). */
+#define SOH_LENS_TEX_CTR 63.5f
+#define SOH_LENS_TEX_RAD 54.8f
+/* Vanilla's own proportion of the vertical HALF field, re-derived:
+ * (54.8 / 128) * (2*LENS_MASK_HEIGHT * SCREEN_HEIGHT /
+ * (SCREEN_HEIGHT - 2*LENS_MASK_OFFSET_T)) / (SCREEN_HEIGHT / 2) = 0.7828. */
+#define SOH_LENS_VR_FRAC 0.7828f
+/* The quad's half extent, in multiples of its distance. The frustum's own
+ * widest tangent on this device is 1.7321, so 3.0 cannot be reached by any
+ * corner of the view at any head roll; the flat red field extends by the tile's
+ * clamp, so oversize costs nothing but the two triangles. */
+#define SOH_LENS_VR_EXTENT 3.0f
+/* Texture coordinates are s16 in S10.5, and the tile origin (96 in s, 56 in t)
+ * is added before the shift -- so the largest texel offset that cannot overflow
+ * is (32767/32) - 96 - 63.5 = 864. 850 keeps a margin there while still
+ * leaving the quad wider than the frustum's own widest tangent at the tightest
+ * legal dials (lensscale 0.3 into a narrow field); at anything a headset
+ * actually reports the cap is not reached at all and H is the full 3 * D. */
+#define SOH_LENS_TEX_MAX 850.0f
+
+static Vtx sSohVrLensVtx[4];
+
+/* The vertical half-tangent of the eye's field. ONE display list is rasterised
+ * into BOTH eyes, so this cannot be per-eye: the max is taken, which errs
+ * towards a slightly larger circle rather than a clipped one. */
+static f32 Actor_VrLensTanV(void) {
+    f32 sohTv = 0.0f;
+    s32 sohEye;
+    s32 sohI;
+
+    for (sohEye = 0; sohEye < 2; sohEye++) {
+        for (sohI = 2; sohI < 4; sohI++) {
+            f32 sohA = gSohVREyeTan[sohEye][sohI];
+
+            if (sohA < 0.0f) {
+                sohA = -sohA;
+            }
+            if (sohA > sohTv) {
+                sohTv = sohA;
+            }
+        }
+    }
+    if (!(sohTv > 0.05f) || (sohTv > 8.0f)) {
+        sohTv = 1.0f; /* the shell has not published yet, or published nonsense */
+    }
+    return sohTv;
+}
+
+static void Actor_VrLensQuad(GraphicsContext* gfxCtx, s32 sohTinted) {
+    f32 sohScale;
+    f32 sohTint;
+    f32 sohRemain;
+    f32 sohWs;
+    f32 sohDist;
+    f32 sohD;
+    f32 sohTv;
+    f32 sohRc;
+    f32 sohK;
+    f32 sohH;
+    f32 sohOff;
+    f32 sohLen;
+    Vec3f sohFwd;
+    Vec3f sohUp;
+    Vec3f sohRight;
+    Vec3f sohUp2;
+    Vec3f sohAt;
+    s32 sohPasses;
+    s32 sohPass;
+    s32 sohAlpha;
+    s32 sohS0;
+    s32 sohS1;
+    s32 sohT0;
+    s32 sohT1;
+    s32 sohI;
+
+    /* --- the head's basis, in game units (overlay 0037's publication) ----- */
+    sohFwd.x = gSohVRCamFwd[0];
+    sohFwd.y = gSohVRCamFwd[1];
+    sohFwd.z = gSohVRCamFwd[2];
+    sohLen = sqrtf((sohFwd.x * sohFwd.x) + (sohFwd.y * sohFwd.y) + (sohFwd.z * sohFwd.z));
+    if (!(sohLen > 1e-4f)) {
+        return;
+    }
+    sohFwd.x /= sohLen;
+    sohFwd.y /= sohLen;
+    sohFwd.z /= sohLen;
+    sohUp.x = gSohVRCamUp[0];
+    sohUp.y = gSohVRCamUp[1];
+    sohUp.z = gSohVRCamUp[2];
+    /* right = fwd x up, up2 = right x fwd. FULLY 3D on purpose: a head roll
+     * must roll the quad, or the circle shears into an oval. The mask is
+     * radially symmetric, so which way round the cross product goes is
+     * unobservable. */
+    sohRight.x = (sohFwd.y * sohUp.z) - (sohFwd.z * sohUp.y);
+    sohRight.y = (sohFwd.z * sohUp.x) - (sohFwd.x * sohUp.z);
+    sohRight.z = (sohFwd.x * sohUp.y) - (sohFwd.y * sohUp.x);
+    sohLen = sqrtf((sohRight.x * sohRight.x) + (sohRight.y * sohRight.y) + (sohRight.z * sohRight.z));
+    if (!(sohLen > 1e-4f)) {
+        return; /* looking exactly along up: no basis, and no frame to draw */
+    }
+    sohRight.x /= sohLen;
+    sohRight.y /= sohLen;
+    sohRight.z /= sohLen;
+    sohUp2.x = (sohRight.y * sohFwd.z) - (sohRight.z * sohFwd.y);
+    sohUp2.y = (sohRight.z * sohFwd.x) - (sohRight.x * sohFwd.z);
+    sohUp2.z = (sohRight.x * sohFwd.y) - (sohRight.y * sohFwd.x);
+
+    /* --- the distance, the circle, and the texture rate ------------------- */
+    sohWs = gSohVRWorldScale;
+    if (!(sohWs > 1.0f)) {
+        sohWs = 34.0f;
+    }
+    sohDist = gSohVRLensDist;
+    if (!(sohDist > 0.4f)) {
+        sohDist = 0.4f;
+    }
+    if (sohDist > 4.0f) {
+        sohDist = 4.0f;
+    }
+    sohD = sohDist * sohWs;
+
+    sohScale = gSohVRLensScale;
+    if (!(sohScale > 0.3f)) {
+        sohScale = 0.3f;
+    }
+    if (sohScale > 2.0f) {
+        sohScale = 2.0f;
+    }
+
+    sohTv = Actor_VrLensTanV();
+    sohRc = SOH_LENS_VR_FRAC * sohD * sohTv * sohScale;
+    if (!(sohRc > 1e-3f)) {
+        return;
+    }
+    sohK = SOH_LENS_TEX_RAD / sohRc;
+    sohH = SOH_LENS_VR_EXTENT * sohD;
+    if ((sohH * sohK) > SOH_LENS_TEX_MAX) {
+        sohH = SOH_LENS_TEX_MAX / sohK; /* s16 texture coordinates, not taste */
+    }
+    sohOff = sohH * sohK;
+
+    sohAt.x = gSohVRCamEye[0] + (sohFwd.x * sohD);
+    sohAt.y = gSohVRCamEye[1] + (sohFwd.y * sohD);
+    sohAt.z = gSohVRCamEye[2] + (sohFwd.z * sohD);
+
+    /* Fast3D subtracts the tile origin and adds half a texel to a TRIANGLE's
+     * coordinates (it does not to a rect's), so both are undone here. */
+    sohS0 = (s32)(((SOH_LENS_TEX_CTR - sohOff - 0.5f) + (f32)(SCREEN_WIDTH / 2 - LENS_MASK_WIDTH)) * 32.0f);
+    sohS1 = (s32)(((SOH_LENS_TEX_CTR + sohOff - 0.5f) + (f32)(SCREEN_WIDTH / 2 - LENS_MASK_WIDTH)) * 32.0f);
+    sohT0 = (s32)(((SOH_LENS_TEX_CTR - sohOff - 0.5f) + (f32)(SCREEN_HEIGHT / 2 - LENS_MASK_HEIGHT)) * 32.0f);
+    sohT1 = (s32)(((SOH_LENS_TEX_CTR + sohOff - 0.5f) + (f32)(SCREEN_HEIGHT / 2 - LENS_MASK_HEIGHT)) * 32.0f);
+
+    /* Local space: the matrix below puts the origin at sohAt. */
+    for (sohI = 0; sohI < 4; sohI++) {
+        f32 sohSx = ((sohI & 1) != 0) ? sohH : -sohH;
+        f32 sohSy = ((sohI & 2) != 0) ? -sohH : sohH;
+
+        sSohVrLensVtx[sohI].v.ob[0] = (s16)((sohRight.x * sohSx) + (sohUp2.x * sohSy));
+        sSohVrLensVtx[sohI].v.ob[1] = (s16)((sohRight.y * sohSx) + (sohUp2.y * sohSy));
+        sSohVrLensVtx[sohI].v.ob[2] = (s16)((sohRight.z * sohSx) + (sohUp2.z * sohSy));
+        sSohVrLensVtx[sohI].v.flag = 0;
+        sSohVrLensVtx[sohI].v.tc[0] = (s16)(((sohI & 1) != 0) ? sohS1 : sohS0);
+        sSohVrLensVtx[sohI].v.tc[1] = (s16)(((sohI & 2) != 0) ? sohT1 : sohT0);
+        sSohVrLensVtx[sohI].v.cn[0] = 255;
+        sSohVrLensVtx[sohI].v.cn[1] = 255;
+        sSohVrLensVtx[sohI].v.cn[2] = 255;
+        sSohVrLensVtx[sohI].v.cn[3] = 255;
+    }
+
+    OPEN_DISPS(gfxCtx);
+
+    /* The same tile as the screen-space path, so the depth site's combiner --
+     * which the caller owns and which reads TEXEL0 -- sees the same texels. */
+    gDPLoadTextureBlock(POLY_XLU_DISP++, gLensOfTruthMaskTex, G_IM_FMT_I, G_IM_SIZ_8b, LENS_MASK_WIDTH,
+                        LENS_MASK_HEIGHT, 0, G_TX_MIRROR | G_TX_CLAMP, G_TX_MIRROR | G_TX_CLAMP, 6, 6, G_TX_NOLOD,
+                        G_TX_NOLOD);
+    gDPSetTileSize(POLY_XLU_DISP++, G_TX_RENDERTILE, (SCREEN_WIDTH / 2 - LENS_MASK_WIDTH) << 2,
+                   (SCREEN_HEIGHT / 2 - LENS_MASK_HEIGHT) << 2, (SCREEN_WIDTH / 2 + LENS_MASK_WIDTH - 1) << 2,
+                   (SCREEN_HEIGHT / 2 + LENS_MASK_HEIGHT - 1) << 2);
+
+    /* The anchor rides a RECORDED matrix, so LUS glides the quad at render rate
+     * between two tick-sampled head poses instead of stepping it. Pushed and
+     * popped on both stacks: this function runs in the middle of the frame's
+     * own matrix work and owes it the stack it was handed. Repeated keys are
+     * matched by ORDINAL by frame_interpolation.cpp, so all three lens sites
+     * may share one. */
+    Matrix_Push();
+    FrameInterpolation_RecordOpenChild((const void*)sSohVrLensVtx, 0);
+    Matrix_Translate(sohAt.x, sohAt.y, sohAt.z, MTXMODE_NEW);
+    gSPMatrix(POLY_XLU_DISP++, MATRIX_NEWMTX(gfxCtx), G_MTX_MODELVIEW | G_MTX_LOAD | G_MTX_PUSH);
+    FrameInterpolation_RecordCloseChild();
+    /* Culling and lighting off: a two-triangle billboard with no normals. Every
+     * Gfx_SetupDL_* the frame runs afterwards loads both back (z_rcp.c). */
+    gSPClearGeometryMode(POLY_XLU_DISP++, G_CULL_BOTH | G_LIGHTING);
+    gSPTexture(POLY_XLU_DISP++, 0xFFFF, 0xFFFF, 0, G_TX_RENDERTILE, G_ON);
+    gSPVertex(POLY_XLU_DISP++, (uintptr_t)&sSohVrLensVtx[0], 4, 0);
+
+    sohTint = gSohVRLensTint;
+    if (!(sohTint > 1.0f)) {
+        sohTint = 1.0f;
+    }
+    if (sohTint > 6.0f) {
+        sohTint = 6.0f;
+    }
+    sohPasses = (sohTinted != 0) ? (s32)(sohTint + 0.98f) : 1;
+    for (sohPass = 0; sohPass < sohPasses; sohPass++) {
+        if (sohTinted != 0) {
+            /* Identical to the screen-space path: colour = PRIMITIVE, alpha =
+             * TEXEL0 * PRIMITIVE, first pass at full alpha, centre clear by
+             * construction because the mask is 0 there. */
+            sohRemain = sohTint - (f32)sohPass;
+            sohAlpha = (sohRemain >= 1.0f) ? 255 : (s32)(255.0f * sohRemain);
+            if (sohAlpha < 1) {
+                sohAlpha = 1;
+            }
+            gDPSetCombineLERP(POLY_XLU_DISP++, 0, 0, 0, PRIMITIVE, TEXEL0, 0, PRIMITIVE, 0, 0, 0, 0, PRIMITIVE, TEXEL0,
+                              0, PRIMITIVE, 0);
+            gDPSetPrimColor(POLY_XLU_DISP++, 0, 0, 255, 0, 0, (u8)sohAlpha);
+        }
+        gSP2Triangles(POLY_XLU_DISP++, 0, 1, 2, 0, 2, 1, 3, 0);
+        gSohVRLensDraws = gSohVRLensDraws + 1;
+    }
+    if (sohTinted != 0) {
+        gSohVRLensPasses = sohPasses;
+    }
+    gSPPopMatrix(POLY_XLU_DISP++, G_MTX_MODELVIEW);
+    gDPPipeSync(POLY_XLU_DISP++);
+
+    CLOSE_DISPS(gfxCtx);
+    Matrix_Pop();
+}
